## Risks  

- **Privacy Erosion** – Every tap, swipe, and app permission creates a data trail. Companies aggregate location, contacts, and usage patterns, often sharing them with third‑party advertisers or, in some jurisdictions, government agencies. Data breaches can expose personal photos, financial details, and health information.  
- **Screen‑Time Addiction** – Persistent notifications and infinite scroll designs trigger dopamine loops. Studies link excessive screen time to reduced attention span, sleep disturbances, anxiety, and depression, especially among teens.  
- **Digital Eye Strain & Physical Health** – Prolonged exposure to blue light leads to eye fatigue, dry‑eye syndrome, and disrupted circadian rhythms. Poor posture while scrolling can cause neck (“text neck”) and shoulder problems.  
- **E‑Waste Accumulation** – Rapid device turnover fuels a global e‑waste crisis. Toxic components (lead, mercury, cadmium) leach into soil and water when phones are improperly discarded, harming ecosystems and human health.  

---

## Ethics  

- **Informed Consent & Data Transparency** – Users rarely read lengthy privacy policies. Ethically, firms should present clear, jargon‑free summaries of what data is collected, why, and how it will be used, offering granular opt‑out options.  
- **Design for Well‑Being vs. Persuasive Design** – Dark‑pattern tactics (e.g., “keep scrolling” prompts) prioritize engagement over user health. Ethical design mandates features like default “do‑not‑disturb” periods, usage dashboards, and easy access to account deletion.  
- **Equitable Access & Digital Divide** – Aggressive monetization can marginalize low‑income users, who may be forced into cheaper, less secure devices or data plans, exacerbating socioeconomic gaps.  
- **Responsibility for E‑Waste** – Manufacturers have an ethical duty to adopt circular‑economy models: modular designs for easy repair, take‑back programs, and use of recyclable materials.  

